  "live_records": {
    "q-97741": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97741",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number obtained when picking a real number between $0$ and $1$ at random.\n\nII. The number obtained when picking an integer between $20$ and $30$ at random.\n\nIII. The number of incidents that occur on a particular road in a month.",
      "worked_solution": "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are infinitely many real numbers between $0$ and $1,$ and so the support is not finite.\n\n- Random variable II can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are a finite number of integers between $20$ and $30,$ and since the selection is made at random, each integer has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the number of incidents can be any positive integer, and each outcome is not equally likely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
