    "q-148395": {
      "dom_id": "step-q148395",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number obtained when picking a ball from a bag with five balls numbered from $3$ to $7$ at random.\n\nII. The outcome obtained when throwing a coin biased toward landing on heads.\n\nIII. The color obtained when choosing a ball from a bag containing $5$ blue and $5$ red balls at random.",
      "worked_solution": "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. There are a finite number of balls, and since the selection is made at random, each ball has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable II cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Since the coin is not fair, each outcome is not equally likely, because there is a higher probability of getting heads.\n\n- Random variable III can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are a finite number of distinct colors, and since the selection is made at random, each color has an equal probability of being picked.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
