      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number of cars that enter a parking lot in an hour.\n\nII. The letter obtained when picking a letter from the alphabet at random.\n\nIII. The amount of time, in minutes, between successive visits to a website.",
      "worked_solution": "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the number of cars can be any positive integer. Moreover, each number of cars is not equally likely.\n\n- Random variable II can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because there are a finite number of letters in the alphabet, and since the selection is random, each letter has an equal probability of being selected.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the amount of time between events is a real number, so the support is not finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
