    "q-97652": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97652",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Ann and Steven are playing a card game. Ann chooses a card at random from a deck of cards labeled $15$ through $40.$ If the number she picks is greater or equal to $20$ and less than $25,$ Ann wins, otherwise Steven wins. What is the probability that Ann wins the game?",
      "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the card number Ann picks. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{15,16,…,40\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(20\\le X<25).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{15,16,…,40\\},$ and the set $S=\\{15,16,…,40\\}$ has $|S|=40-15+1=26$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{26}, & x=15,16,…,40 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $20\\le X<25$ are $20,21,22,23,24.$ There are $5$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(20\\le X<25)=5\\cdot \\frac{1}{26}=\\frac{5}{26}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
