    "q-97628": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97628",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "In a math test, a student has to answer a multiple-choice question, and they decide to select an answer at random. The answers are labeled from $1$ to $5.$ What is the probability that the student picks one of the first $2$ answers?",
      "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the selected answer. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{1,2,…,5\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X\\le 2).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{1,2,…,5\\},$ and the set $S=\\{1,2,…,5\\}$ has $|S|=5-1+1=5$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}, & x=1,2,…,5 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X\\le 2$ are $1,2.$ There are $2$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 2)=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
