      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Jennifer's marbles are labeled with numbers $7$ through $22.$ If Jennifer chooses a marble at random, what is the probability that the marble number she picks is greater than or equal to $10$ and less than $17?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the number on the chosen marble. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{7,8,…,22\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(10\\le X<17).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{7,8,…,22\\},$ and the set $S=\\{7,8,…,22\\}$ has $|S|=22-7+1=16$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}, & x=7,8,…,22 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $10\\le X<17$ are $10,11,12,13,14,15,16.$ There are $7$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(10\\le X<17)=7\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}=\\frac{7}{16}.\n$$",
